! Proposed Definitions And Examples


!! Proposed Definition of ''bu'a''

;__bu'a__ (GOhA): Logically quantified selbri 1.
** Keywords: 

!! Examples of ''bu'a'' Usage
doi .eimis. pe'i ro bu'a zo'u lo prenu cu ka'e jai bu'a%%%
''Amy, my opinion is that for any state/action/property/thing X, (some) people are capable of being/doing/having something to do with X.''%%%

ganai do djica lonu bu'a gi gauko bu'a%%%
''If there is something you want to happen, make it happen.''%%%

zo poi bu'a zo noi .ije zo voi bu'a ma%%%
''What is to “voi” as “noi” is to “poi”?''%%%


!! Proposed Definition of ''bu'e''

;__bu'e__ (GOhA): Logically quantified selbri 2.
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''bu'e'' Usage
xu ro bu'a ro bu'e zo'u ganai bu'a seni'i bu'e gi ge bu'a gi bu'e

xu ro nu bu'a cu du'u bu'e%%%
''Is every event a predication?''

!! Proposed Definition of ''bu'i''

;__bu'i__ (GOhA): Logically quantified selbri 3.
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''bu'i'' Usage

ro bu'a su'o bu'e su'o bu'i zo'u ge bu'a gi'a bu'e gi'a bu'i gi co'e%%%
For every P, Some Q and Some R: both P or Q or R; and something unspecified.%%%
''For any proposition, there is at least two propositions such that both what we were talking about earlier and at least one of the propositions is true.''%%%
__This translation needs checked; unsure about the logical connectives here.__
*I think the translator got confused by the contrast between Lojban-prefix and English-infix. It's "P and (Q or (R or something))". The first operator has the highest scope. That said, I believe the translation should be something like this: ''For any proposition, there exists at least one proposition such that there exists at least one proposition such that the first proposition is true and one of the second two propositions or what we were talking about earlier is true.'' --mi'e zort

!! Proposed Definition of ''co'e''

;__co'e__ (GOhA): Unspecified selbri. Represents a selbri whose exact meaning is unimportant/obvious.
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''co'e'' Usage
i ko na co'e sei le nolraitru cu cusku%%%
''“Don't,” said the king.''

le so'u kurji be le bangu co'e pe la tolkien cu se xebni le drata sarji be le bi'unai bangu%%%
The few custodians of Tolkien's language stuff were despised by the other supporters of that language.

nabmi mi lenu co'e la emaks%%%
''I have problems with Emacs.''

ju'i clsn ko co'e%%%
''Shoulson! Do it.''

!! Proposed Definition of ''du''

;__du__ (GOhA): Equal sign. x1 equals x2, x3, etc. Used for math and also to say that multiple different sumti refer to the same thing.
** Keywords: equal

!! Examples of ''du'' Usage
doi norsmu li zepamu pi'i rexamu du li xo%%%
''Norsmu, what is 715 × 265?''

y la cizra du la entrop%%%
''Er... Cizra and Entrop is the same person.''

ri du ma%%%
"What is ~np~[doing what you just said]~/np~?''
!! Proposed Definition of ''go'a''

;__go'a__ (GOhA): Repeats a recent bridi (usually not the last 2).
** Keywords: recent bridi

!! Examples of ''go'a'' Usage
<xorxes> xu ro cmene be lo gugde ba se vasru la jbovlaste%%%
<Broca> mi troci tu'a lo glico pavbauvlacku%%%
<Broca> a'o go'a%%%
''<xorxes> Will all names of countries be in Jbovlaste?''%%%
''<Broca> I try with an English monolingual dictionary.''%%%
''<Broca> Hopefully [all names of countries will be in Jbovlaste].''

!! Proposed Definition of ''go'e''

;__go'e__ (GOhA): penultimate bridi -- pro-bridi: repeats the next to last bridi --
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''go'e'' Usage

na go'e doi tomoj .i mi damba la lindar%%%
''No, Tomoj. I'm fighting with Lindar.''

!! Proposed Definition of ''go'i''

;__go'i__ (GOhA): last bridi -- pro-bridi: preceding bridi; in answer to a yes/no question, repeats the claim, meaning yes --
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''go'i'' Usage

.y. mi na go'i .i ku'i ru'a lo samtci na ka'e djica%%%
''Uh, I don't. But I presume that a computer program isn't able to want.''

!! Proposed Definition of ''go'ira'o''

;__go'ira'o__ (GOhA*): true for me too -- pro-bridi: preceding bridi; repeats previous bridi, forcing any pro-sumti to be reinterpreted with respect to the new speaker. ra'o can be appended to any cmavo of GOhA, not just "go'i".

** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''go'ira'o'' Usage

do go'ira'o .i ku'i ma na selcnino la lojban%%%
''You also do that, but to whom isn't Lojban new?''

<djancak> mi nelci lo nu tavla bau la lojban%%%
<gunspoja> go'i ra'o%%%
''I like talking in Lojban.''%%%
''Me too.''%%%

!! Proposed Definition of ''go'o''

;__go'o__ (GOhA): future bridi -- pro-bridi: repeats a future bridi, normally the next one --
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''go'o'' Usage

xu do go'o .i mi tavla fo la .lojban.%%%
''Do you? I talk in Lojban.''

!! Proposed Definition of ''go'u''

;__go'u__ (GOhA): earlier bridi -- pro-bridi: repeats a remote past bridi --
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''go'u'' Usage

mi djuno lo nu do ka'e go'u%%%
''I know you're capable of doing that thing somebody said a while ago.''

!! Proposed Definition of ''mo''

;__mo__ (GOhA): bridi ? -- pro-bridi: asks for a selbri to fill in its spot.
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''mo'' Usage

lo su'u do mo cu cinri mi%%%
''What are you doing that is interesting to me?''

do mo prenu%%%
"What kind of person are you?"

!! Proposed Definition of ''nago'i''

;__nago'i__ (GOhA*): deny last bridi -- selbri: repeats preceding bridi while denying it as false; pro-assign values are unchanged --
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''nago'i'' Usage

artificial:%%%
.i doi .cmen. do plise vau xu
.i .oi na go'i

Cmen, are you an apple?
Argh, no.

xu do merko%%%
na go'i%%%
mi dotco%%%
''Are you American?''%%%
''No.''%%%
''I'm German.''

!! Proposed Definition of ''nei''

;__nei__ (GOhA): current bridi -- pro-bridi: repeats the current bridi --
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''nei'' Usage

du'o nai lo mamta be do mi gletu lo jai du'o nai nei%%%
''Unbeknownst to your mother, I had sex with someone who was unaware of it.''

ko geirgau le tadni poi na nei ke'a%%%
"Make those students whom you have not made happy, happy."

!! Issues
* [http://groups.google.com/group/lojban/browse_thread/thread/ade282a0c1aaf6d5|na nei ?]

!! Proposed Definition of ''no'a''

;__no'a__ (GOhA): next outer bridi -- pro-bridi: repeats the bridi in which this one is embedded --
** Keywords:

!! Examples of ''no'a'' Usage

mi gleki lo nu no'a %%%
"I'm happy about being happy."%%%

le la turnianskis selru'a be fi la lojban zo'u la lojban kulnu nutli gi'e satci  fi'o nafmupli fe'u lo nu ly na no'a %%%
''Turniansky's postulate about Lojban: Lojban is culturally neutral and exact, with the exception that Lojban isn't.''

! Notes

Can bu'a be "na klama"?  That makes things pretty fucking weird; {ro bu'a ro da ro de zo'u da bu'a de .i jo nai da na bu'a de} ''is not actually true'', because "da na klama de" and "da na na klama de" are both true.  Do note, however, that {da ja'a bu'a de .i jo nai da na bu'a de} fixes it.

No, of course not.  "na" is only syntactically part of the selbri; semantically it applies to the whole bridi.  --jcowan

But what happens when bu'a == narbroda? --latros

narbroda is a positive selbri: it is true of exactly those tuples that broda is false of.  But in any case, the original claim is bogus: "ro da ro de zo'u da na klama de" is true, because it means "naku ro da ro de zo'u da klama de" (it's true that it is not the case that for every x and every y, x goes to y") and "ro da ro de zo'u da na na klama de" is false, because it means "naku naku ro da ro de zo'u da klama de" which in turn means "ro da ro de klama de", and it is not true that every x goes to every y).  So the claim of "pretty fucking weird" seems to me to be incorrect.  Multiple consecutive {na} in the selbri cancels out; it's only when you say {na go'i} to a bridi containing {na} that the two are assimilated into just one.  --jcowan

There is an issue with bu'a, which also extends to selbri in general to a lesser extent. It is essentially not possible to treat selbri as sumti; that is, to consider a predicate as a predicate logic variable. This is especially relevant to bu'a, because we might want to assert the existence of a predicate which satisfies a certain predicate and is also the selbri of a bridi involving certain terbri. An example when this would be warranted is [http://www.lojban.org/tiki/tiki-index.php?page=Axiom+of+stratified+comprehension--Mekso+example|here]. The English sentence "For all stratified predicates P, the set {x : P(x)} exists" ostensibly cannot be translated in the same style; that is, it is apparently not possible to assert that a predicate satisfies a relation in the prenex and then use it as the selbri of a bridi. You can say {ro da poi ke'a selbri gi'e ... zo'u}, and you can say {ro bu'a zo'u}, but you cannot do something that does both things.
*rlpowell's proposed solution appears to be to import {da poi ke'a selbri} from the prenex and then treat that as bu'a, leading to {ro da poi ke'a selbri gi'e multersenta zo'u lo'i bu'a cu zasti}. This seems a bit..."destructive", to me (mi'e latros), since now I am fairly sure that something is horribly wrong with {da bu'a}.
*My proposed solution is to introduce sumti-to-predicate and predicate-to-sumti cmavo. It may sound like both of these exist, but I am fairly sure they do not. Using, say {me'au}, {me'au ko'a} would be "x1 ko'a x2 x3 ..." where ko'a is a predicate. Then using, say, {me'ei}, {me'ei broda} would be "broda-as-an-abstract-predicate". Then when quantifying over selbri in prenexes, you would use {ro me'ei bu'a}. The previous example then becomes: {ro me'ei bu'a poi ke'a multersenta zo'u lo'i bu'a cu zasti}. This removes the horrible selbri quantification hack, in that {ro bu'a zo'u} would be essentially no different from {ro da zo'u}, except that it would import {lo bu'a} instead of {da}. (It replaces it with another hack, but I would say this one is less bizarre.) In principle {me'ei broda} could be "x1 is broda-as-an-abstract-predicate", but I can't think of how this would ever be used beyond {lo me'ei broda}.

{bu'a} is treated weirdly: it's a selbri normally, but in the prenex it is effectively a sumti.  The reason this works is that although {ro bu'a} is syntactically a quantifier+sumti-tail type of description, it is taken to mean "for all P". --jcowan

